            "<ol><li><strong>Recall Answer:</strong> Approximately 20% (nearly one-fifth) of the 3 million people living in the West Bank are Jewish Israeli citizens.</li><li><strong>Explain Answer:</strong> Israel's victory in the 1967 Six-Day War resulted in its military capturing the West Bank beyond the 1948 Green Line. While the Israeli government debated what to do with the territory and its 1.1 million Palestinian inhabitants, many Israeli civilians viewed the military victory as a religious sign that Jews were meant to return to their ancient spiritual homeland (&quot;Judea and Samaria&quot;). As a result, civilians began establishing communities in the West Bank without government permission. This grassroots movement created a permanent civilian presence on the ground that future government policy had to accommodate, directly birthing the settler movement.</li><li><strong>Challenge Answer:</strong> The narrator maintains an objective, neutral tone by explicitly framing the conflict through &quot;two different narratives.&quot; To represent the settlers' perspective, the narrator uses terms emphasizing emotion and heritage, such as moving to land with &quot;deep history and spiritual significance.&quot; In contrast, to represent the majority international/Palestinian perspective, the narrator uses critical legal and geopolitical terms, such as &quot;colonizing the land,&quot; &quot;illegal occupation,&quot; and creating an &quot;obstacle to... peace.&quot; By attributing these views clearly to each group without declaring one side definitive, the narrator creates a balanced analytical framework.</li></ol>",
